// FAQ Items
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const faqs = [
    {
        q: "How long do car tyres typically last in Dubai?",
        a: "In Dubai, passenger car tyres typically last 30,000 to 50,000 km, while heavier SUVs often see 25,000 to 40,000 km, depending on driving habits, mileage, and maintenance. However, tyre age is just as important as mileage. In the UAE's hot climate, rubber compounds harden and oxidize over time. For annual RTA vehicle testing, tyres older than 5 years from their DOT manufacturing date generally fail roadworthiness checks and must be replaced regardless of remaining tread depth."
    },
    {
        q: "What tyre pressure should I maintain during the Dubai summer?",
        a: "Always maintain the cold tyre inflation pressure specified by your vehicle manufacturer. This is found on the placard inside the driver's door jamb or in the owner's manual. Do not guess a universal figure or intentionally under-inflate tyres to compensate for summer heat; under-inflated tyres generate dangerous internal heat during highway driving. Always check tyre pressures when the tyres are cold (parked for at least three hours or driven less than 2 km)."
    },
    {
        q: "Can a punctured tyre be repaired, or does it need replacement?",
        a: "Whether a puncture can be repaired depends on its size, location, and the tyre manufacturer's guidance. A puncture can generally be repaired if it is small (typically up to 6 mm) and located within the central three-quarters of the tread crown, and the tyre was not driven flat. Safe repair requires demounting the tyre to inspect internal casing damage and installing a combination patch-and-plug. Punctures in the tyre shoulder or sidewall cannot be repaired safely and require tyre replacement."
    },
    {
        q: "How do I read my car tyre size and specifications?",
        a: "Tyre sizes are stamped on the sidewall in a sequence such as 225/55 R17 97W. Here, 225 is the tyre width in millimetres, 55 is the aspect ratio (sidewall height as 55% of width), R denotes radial construction, and 17 is the wheel rim diameter in inches. 97 is the load index (maximum load capacity per tyre, 730 kg), and W is the speed rating (up to 270 km/h). Replacement tyres must match or exceed your vehicle manufacturer's specified load index and speed rating."
    },
    {
        q: "How often should wheel alignment and balancing be performed?",
        a: "Wheel balancing should be performed whenever new tyres are fitted, after a puncture repair, or if you feel steering wheel or seat vibration at motorway speeds. Wheel alignment should be checked every 15,000 to 20,000 km, whenever you install a new set of tyres, or if you notice your car pulling to one side or tyres wearing unevenly on the shoulders."
    },
    {
        q: "Are budget tyres safe for driving in the UAE?",
        a: "Budget tyres are safe for UAE roads provided they carry official GSO conformity marking and appropriate temperature ratings for high-temperature climates. However, premium and mid-range tyres generally provide shorter braking distances, quieter highway cruising, and longer tread life. Regardless of brand, always ensure the tyres match the load index and speed rating specified for your vehicle."
    }
];

